@voznesenskym: If users have library code where they have custom types, where they want dynamo to provide some kind of custom behavior, what should that extension point look like?

Motivating example: @drisspg wrote some SDPA structure in C++ that is ostensibly either third-party like, or higher level than most torch primitives. 

1) Don't offer it, too brittle and clumsy
2) Add an extension point to where we do type-dispatch and a registration API that lets folks provide their own VariableTracker impl (This makes VT interface publish-ish, which I dislike a lot)
3) Add an extension point to where structures/types can be decomped into python structures/types a la ops

A few various library authors have asked for this, and while some like numpy are ubiquitous enough to become first class citizens, it certainly isnt the right path for all..

@suo: this sounds like torchbind/c++ custom classes?
@suo: it is indeed brittle and clumsy, but is extremely useful lol

@voznesenskym: Arguably less brittle and clumsy than having a smattering of layering violating/third party structures across all of dynamo for providing custom mapping whatever is the lib-du-jour. 😉 more brittle and clumsy than doing nothing I guess

@mlazos: I like 2, but I don't see a good way to do it, as you allude to, I do consider Driss' stuff less third party since it does live in torch.* if that matters, https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/117207 

@anijain2305: I guess here user would want control even in deeper layers of torch.compile like AOTAutograd and possibly Inductor, and not just Dynamo?

@voznesenskym: Imo as long as you provide a recipe to graph capture it soundly, lowering should Just Work (tm) but maybe there are cases where thats not true? 

For custom data types, it seems to be true, at least.

@ezyang:

I think it's different than torchbind in that user defined C++ structs may not ever show in the graph. A smaller extension point surface that we should explore first is "pure" data structures without any embedded tensors which cannot be mutated, so we can just constant fold them. Maybe figure out the minimal api needed to deal with mutation too. I don't want us to commit to a stable api for variable tracker, not this year anyway, I would say that we should liberally accept patches for VT support for external libraries, but this is actually kind of useless unless we're willing to test them too.

Another extension point surface which I think is possible is one where you write a reference implementation in Python and we use that to work out how the C++ one would have behaved. We have this for functions (polyfill) and I think this is a very reasonable and stable extension point - oh, that's voz's (3).



