### 🐛 Describe the bug

Consider the program below:

```python
import torch

d = {}

@torch.compile(backend="eager", fullgraph=True)
def fn(x, key):
    if key not in d:
        d[key] = 1
    return x.sin()

x = torch.randn(2)
fn(x, "torch")

with torch.compiler.set_stance("fail_on_recompile"):
    fn(x, "torch")
```

This code uses a common lazy-initialization pattern found in real-world libraries (e.g. [einops](https://github.com/arogozhnikov/einops/blob/43a12ee010a844f3ad57bf404d27e4ee2d151131/einops/_backends.py#L17-L34)). However, under `torch.compile`, it triggers a recompilation on the second execution due to the global dict mutation. 

For einops this can be "fixed" by initializing the library beforehand, but it would be good if we could have a way to prevent this from happening.  

