### 🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch

### The feature

PyTorch recently gained support for [CUDA Graph compatible `torch.cond`](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/168912). This feature request is a generalization of `torch.cond` to support N-way branching during stream capture. Just like `torch.cond`, `torch.switch` maps nicely to [switch type CUDA graph conditional nodes](https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/dynamic-control-flow-in-cuda-graphs-with-conditional-nodes/#conditional_switch_nodes). The API would be identical to [jax.lax.switch](https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/_autosummary/jax.lax.switch.html).

### Motivation

[PyTorch docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/user_guide/torch_compiler/torch.compiler_cudagraph_trees.html#cudagraph-trees-integration) demonstrate how a graph tree is constructed in the presence of a conditional node. For a function containing 4 subgraphs that selects between graph 2 and 3 based on a condition, we would instantiate a graph tree containing two copies of graph 4. Graph 4 needs to be recorded twice because depending on which path was taken, the input memory addresses can change.

```mermaid

flowchart LR;

code["<pre style='text-align:left;'>def func(x):<br>  y = graph_1(x)<br>  if pred(y):<br>    z = graph_2(y)<br>  else:<br>    z = graph_3(y)<br>  return graph_4(y)</pre>"];

subgraph L["Python Control Flow"]
1 --> 2;
1 --> 3;
2 --> 4;
3 --> 4;
end

subgraph R["CUDA Graph Tree"]
_1[1];
_2[2];
_3[3];
_4_1[4.1];
_4_2[4.2];
_1 --> _2 --> _4_1;
_1 --> _3 --> _4_2;
end

code ~~~ L ~~~ R;
```

If we were to have control flow with N-way branching, the downstream graph will be instantiated N times. Adding more control flow in subgraphs will increase the branching multiplicatively.

Fortunately, just like `torch.cond` can avoid this by using CUDA Graph conditional nodes, switch can do the same in CUDA >= 12.8. 

