So the question is how we should manage the numeric precision matter of torch.compile. Currently we have all or nothing or fragmented solutions.
1. Global flag like torch._inductor.config.emulate_precision_casts=True can be used (for testing only?) to preserve the cast globally.
2. If we can identify and root cause the area of the code which cause numeric problem, we can use torch._dynamo.graph_break or torch._dynamo disable compile the region of problem.
3. https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/189334 is proposing a parameter to torch.compile function for specifying precision behanvior.
4. https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/187971 is adding a global flag torch._inductor.config.numerics = "strict" for makes torch.sum reductions bit-for-bit identical to eager

Usually the code region which is prone to larger numeric drift has a pattern (data cast followed by non-linear operations) and it is constrained to some specific layers or regions. These all or nothing global flag are not ideal to be used in production.  While the graph break or disable torch.compile solutions looks like a hack or a walkaround.

The ideally approach to the torch.compile numeric precision behavior is to design a scoped precision related context which makes the compiling process follow a specified precision behaviors within the context, and back to default (performant) mode when exit from the context.  The scoped precision context can be torch.compile independent which means it is part of the model script no matter whether the model is torch.compiled or not . And it takes effect when it is under the compiled path.

For example:

```
with precision_context(precision_casts=True, numerics="strict", ...):
        temb = time_text_embed

```

